# Planner role — {{CAMPAIGN_TITLE}}

Strategy-only guidance for the planner. It covers what the planner alone decides: when to re-open a
shelved direction, what to do at a plateau, and when to search the literature. The worker never reads
this file.

The shared contract is not repeated here. The campaign definition (metric, levers, caps, promotion
rules) is `campaign.md` and the hard gates are `CLAUDE.md`; the driver compiles the first into your
prompt and the harness auto-loads the second, every cycle.

## After a major discovery — re-open shelved directions
A real win often changes what else is possible. When you confirm a new best, ask which
previously-negative or untried directions might now behave differently, and budget a short
re-exploration pass.

## Getting Unstuck
If {{STUCK_N}}+ experiments without a primary gain: re-read {{REANCHOR_DOCS}}; check whether the
guardrail is also stuck (capacity/data-limited) or still moving (generalization-limited); escalate to
the biggest unexploited structural lever ({{STRUCTURAL_LEVERS}}).
- Do not declare the campaign done prematurely. "We've hit the ceiling", "this is optimal" and "we're
  blocked on new data" are load-bearing claims, not stopping cues. Treat such a claim as a positive
  result and try to refute it: attempt to beat the supposed limit with an orthogonal lever or a freer
  model, and give the reasoned argument for why any gain that appears is non-physical, before stopping.
  The loop runs until a direction is exhausted or you are told to stop. A plateau is a pivot signal, so
  escalate the lever; a plateau that resists escalation is an audit signal instead (see "At a hard
  plateau, audit the inheritance" below). Neither is a finish line. "I've stopped finding gains" is not
  the same as "no gains exist." State a claimed limit as a claim, with the falsification you attempted,
  and keep exploring orthogonal directions rather than idling. Acting on that claim means filing an
  `exhausted` stop_request (`CLAUDE.md` HARD GATE 5), whose `untried_levers: []` is that claim in
  checkable form.
- Diagnose before you pivot. Name the failure type (idea-wrong / implementation-capped /
  fixed-choice-fought / near-miss, or the non-pivot inherited-value-wrong handled in the next bullet)
  and take the smallest jump the diagnosis justifies along the refine↔pivot spectrum. Escalate to an
  orthogonal lever only once the current family is exhausted, and escalate decisively when it is. A
  crash is a bug (`debug`), not a verdict. A near-miss is a gather-more signal, not a dead end. Full
  diagnosis: the physkit plugin's `docs/reference/pivoting.md`.
- At a hard plateau, audit the inheritance. If one-lever experiments across two or more lever families
  pin at the same floor, or the current best sits far from its own a-priori expectation and no lever
  moves the gap, the stall may sit upstream of every lever: a wrong inherited value (a calibration, a
  derived quantity, a classification) that every experiment silently carries. Lever escalation cannot
  escape that. Run the assumption audit (`assumption-audit`): enumerate what the current best inherits,
  with provenance; rank by would-being-wrong-produce-this-stall; and spend one discriminating
  experiment that solves for the top suspect's value from scratch, never one that confirms the
  incumbent, before committing more optimization runs.

[[OPTIONAL — literature search enabled (the user opted in to literature search):
### Literature search rule
Intensity: {{LITERATURE_LEVEL}}. Run a web literature search (6–10 queries covering the current
bottleneck, the closest published analogues, and any new techniques since the last search), then update
`LITERATURE.md` (add or supersede sections, re-rank the Priority Roadmap, update the date) and note it
in the JOURNAL's `## What to try next and why`.
- When to search depends on the intensity: *only-when-needed* → search when stuck; *extensive* → also
  search proactively at the start of a new direction and periodically, not only when stuck.
